  "narrative_body": "At a Catholic convention in Würzburg this week, Friedrich Merz told an audience he would not advise his children to live or work in the United States — the most explicit public distancing from Washington by a sitting German chancellor since the war.

The remark followed the American president's attack — calling Mr Merz someone who "knows nothing" — after Mr Merz had said Iran was "humiliating" Washington. A phone call on Friday produced a "good" framing from both sides, with agreement cited on Iran and Ukraine. But Richard Grenell, a senior US adviser, continued his public attacks on Mr Merz after the call, signaling the rupture is structural rather than a misunderstanding to be managed away.

The damage is spreading beyond the bilateral relationship. Three US officials confirmed to AP that 4,000 troops from the Army's 2nd Armored Brigade Combat Team were no longer heading to Poland, canceled by a memo from Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth directing the Joint Chiefs to remove a brigade combat team from Europe. The decision was triggered by the Germany-specific presidential order but reached across the alliance: Republican congressman Don Bacon reported Poland was "blindsided," even as some US military equipment had already arrived in European ports. What began as friction between Berlin and Washington has become a structural European security problem.

Even as it quarreled with Washington, Germany was deepening the alternative track it has been building since the US began pulling back. Boris Pistorius, the defense minister, made a surprise visit to Kyiv and command posts in Zaporizhzhia, watching live drone operations. He and Volodymyr Zelenskyy announced joint development and production of drones covering ranges from under 100 km to 1,500 km — approaching the deterrence range of the Tomahawk missiles Germany can no longer get from Washington. Germany has trained nearly 27,000 Ukrainian soldiers; Mr Pistorius confirmed training will continue after a ceasefire as part of European security guarantees. He called Russia's peace rhetoric a "deception maneuver" and assessed Moscow to be in a "phase of weakness."

That judgment about Russian weakness contrasts with Germany's own capability picture. The Tomahawk stationing plan, agreed by the Scholz government and Washington in 2024, is now formally canceled. The gap is wider than previously understood: the US had also planned to station SM-6 anti-aircraft missiles and hypersonic weapons, none of which will now arrive. Mr Pistorius acknowledged that an official purchase request submitted 18 months ago has gone unanswered, and admitted he does not hold "too much hope" given US stockpile depletion from the Iran war. A single unconfirmed report this week — from one low-tier source — suggested Berlin is exploring Turkish missiles as a substitute; the claim awaits corroboration. Carsten Breuer, the Bundeswehr's Inspector General, used the same Würzburg stage to warn that Russia could be ready to fight the West by 2029. A formal government report submitted to the Bundestag this week designated Russia Germany's "greatest and most direct threat," assessed Russian ballistic missiles as capable of striking "virtually all of Europe," and noted that Russia's 1.5 million-soldier buildup will complete this year. The Ukraine drone partnership is the most actionable alternative track Germany has opened, but it is years from producing deployable systems. The gap between the 2029 threat window and Germany's procurement horizons remains open.

