The coalition's governing legitimacy took another blow when Mr Merz was booed at the trade union confederation's congress in Berlin — specifically during passages on pension and health reform. Frank Werneke, the Ver.di chief, said Mr Merz had set up reform commissions "without any participation of the social partners," a departure from the practice of every chancellor since Kohl. A first Chancellery meeting with unions was conceded for June. The SPD's Lars Klingbeil and Bärbel Bas fared little better at the same event, suggesting the legitimacy crisis spans both governing parties rather than attaching to Mr Merz alone. The income tax relief concept that Mr Klingbeil promised "in weeks" at the coalition's one-year mark has still not been published, leaving a gap at the center of the coalition's economic case to voters.

